Add system-sleep support to the ST accelerometer drivers.

The ST sensor drivers carry no power management at all: struct
i2c_driver/spi_driver have no .pm, and there is no suspend/resume anywhere
under drivers/iio/common/st_sensors/ or drivers/iio/accel/st_accel*.

Across a suspend that actually removes power the chip returns at its reset
defaults.  Everything the driver programmed is gone: ODR, full-scale,
BDU/DAS, axis enables, the data-ready interrupt enable, and -- the part
that is easiest to miss -- the interrupt line polarity.

Observed on a Pine64 PineNote (SC7A20 on i2c, level-triggered through the
rockchip GPIO controller) after its first platform "deep" suspend, and
confirmed to reproduce on both a 6.12 BSP kernel and 7.0.11 -- i.e. it is
inherent to the driver, not to one image.

There are two distinct failures, and fixing only the first converts a loud
one into a silent one:

  a) the interrupt storms.  With a level-triggered DRDY line the threaded
     handler cannot claim the interrupt, it re-asserts forever, and the
     kernel's spurious-interrupt protection disables it permanently:

       irq 73: nobody cared (try booting with the "irqpoll" option)
       CPU: 0 UID: 0 PID: 200 Comm: irq/73-sc7a20-t

  b) the interrupt goes silent.  The chip keeps sampling into an overrun
     and still looks alive in sysfs -- registers read back fine, raw
     channels are readable -- while not one interrupt is ever delivered
     and autorotation is dead.

The fix has four parts:

1. Split the register-programming tail of st_sensors_init_sensor() into
   st_sensors_reinit_hw(), which touches no mutex initialisation and no
   device-tree parsing and is therefore safe to repeat.

2. On resume, reprogram from cached state and re-arm DRDY -- latching
   ->hw_irq_trigger BEFORE calling st_sensors_reinit_hw().  reinit_hw()
   inherits init_sensor()'s "disable DRDY, this might still be enabled
   after reboot" step, and st_sensors_set_dataready_irq() assigns
   ->hw_irq_trigger as a side effect, so a later test of the flag always
   reads false.  The re-arm is then skipped, DRDY stays off, and
   st_sensors_irq_thread() returns IRQ_NONE on every interrupt -- failure
   (a), measured as ~100,000 interrupts in the second after resume.

3. Restore the interrupt polarity in st_sensors_reinit_hw().
   st_sensors_allocate_trigger() is the only place the active-low bit is
   ever written and it runs once at probe, so after a power-removing
   suspend the chip is back to its active-high reset default while the
   GPIO is still configured IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_LOW.  The line reads
   permanently de-asserted -- failure (b).

   Measured on the PineNote (addr_ihl 0x25, mask_ihl 0x02): after a deep
   cycle 0x25 read back 0x00 and the data-ready IRQ counted 0 in 5 s with
   STATUS_REG at 0xff (data ready, all axes overrun).  Writing the bit by
   hand restored exactly 1 Hz, matching the configured ODR, with no storm.

4. Mask the interrupt across the transition with disable_irq()/
   enable_irq(), so the handler cannot run against a chip that is still at
   its reset defaults.  A ->pm_irq_disabled flag keeps the pairing exact
   so the depth counter cannot unbalance if suspend returns early.

A note on (4): an earlier revision carried parts 1 and 4 only and still
stormed, which was attributed to resume_device_irqs() running at the noirq
stage, before driver .resume callbacks.  That revision also contained the
(2) ordering bug, which is sufficient on its own to explain the failure,
so the noirq argument should be read as unproven motivation for (4)
rather than as a measured result.  Part 4 is retained because masking a
level-triggered line across a power-removing transition is correct
regardless, but the defects actually measured were (2) and (3).

